This treats the leading term and yields the condition on $\alpha$ and $\beta$ stated in the lemma. We turn now to the term $J_2$. For $\varphi \in C^1_c(\Rm^{d})$, we use the following representation formula for the fractional Laplacian (see e.g. \cite{stein1}): we have, for any $\alpha \in (0,1)$,
\begin{equation} \label{locfrac}
 D^\alpha(\varphi)(x)=c_{d,\alpha} \int_{\Rm^d} \frac{\varphi(x)-\varphi(y)}{|x-y|^{d+\alpha}} dy.
\end{equation}
Note that there is no need to introduce a principal value in the definition since $\alpha \in (0,1)$ and $\varphi \in C^1$. We then write
